WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.900
Right. I would like to invite Sir Cyril Chantler
to join us for the reports from the committees.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.620
I would like to kick off, if I may, with Ed
and the Board audit committee.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:19.880
I will be brief. We will cover the annual
report and accounts this afternoon, Malcolm.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:25.080
However, in addition to your thanks, I think
we should recognise the fantastic work done

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:30.310
by internal audit and Deloitte over the course
of the last year. We should also recognise

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:35.440
the support we have had from the Department
of Health and indeed the NAO. They have helped

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.079
us get to where we hoped to get to in front
of recess.

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:46.760
I will go back to the Board assurance framework,
which we covered this morning. It is management's

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:52.570
job to identify, manage and mitigate risks.
We, as an audit committee, will look at the

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:57.579
assurance mechanisms. We have taken the view
that in the coming year we want to take some

00:00:57.579 --> 00:01:04.089
deep dives into selective risks there. We
have set out here the areas which we think

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:09.640
are right. However, we would be happy to take
observations from other Board members as to

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.970
their views. We cannot cover everything.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:19.970
The last point I would make is on counter
fraud, error and dispersion or leakage of

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:25.880
money. We are going to go through a process
in the coming year which we started with Paul.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:32.880
We will look at using world class, best practice
analytics. They will be needed to look at

00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:42.079
the way in which we can reduce the incidence
of fraud and error in the NHS.

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:43.360
That is all I have to report.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:50.140
Malcolm Grant: Thank you Ed. Any comments
or questions for Ed? No, thank you.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.189
Victor, authorisation and assurance committee?

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:55.500
Victor Adebowale: I have nothing to bring
to the Board's attention, other than what

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.990
is in the minutes, other than to point out
that myself and Ciaran and quite keen on tackling

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:05.380
the thorny issue of outcome for patients and
communities from the assurance process. However,

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:10.940
I think that is a discussion we are having,
usefully, with Barbara and colleagues.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:16.730
Malcolm Grant: Right. Thank you. Let me then
turn please to Moira. Moira, it is suggested

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:22.160
you might be able to give us a verbal update
from the meeting on 23rd June of commissioning

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:22.660
support?

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:29.440
Moira Gibb: 16th June, rather than the 23rd
June. However, anyway, it was in June.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:30.890
Malcolm Grant: We will get the date right
in a minute.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:36.620
Moira Gibb: Yes, thank you. There are really
three points I would raise and report to the

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:42.209
Board, Malcolm. We had an update on the lead
provider framework, in particular the positive

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:49.209
response we had to that. That also included
the plans to have additional lots to assist

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:58.700
access to niche suppliers. Secondly, we had
a report on CSU performance. We noted they

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:07.040
had gained an additional £96 million of income
on top of the planned surplus of £45 million.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.159
I think that was a very good performance.
My third point is really the context for that

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:17.560
performance. That context is, of course, the
continued change in the architecture of CSUs.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:23.319
We had reports on two: Central Eastern and
North West London. I think they were both

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:29.269
being very well handled. However, nearly 1,200
staff were being affected by that. We should

00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:33.580
not, therefore, fail to recognise again the
scale of change that people are having to

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.280
undergo in CSUs.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.760
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for that.
Let us move on to efficiency controls. Paul?

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:46.230
Paul Baumann: I will give it briefly, Malcolm.
The first page is kind of as it always is.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:51.790
It has the bald numbers in it. On the second
page paragraph three has got bigger than it

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:56.459
has been in previous reports. Basically, every
month up to now, or every Board meeting up

00:03:56.459 --> 00:04:01.060
to now, I have been saying we are making progress
to rationalise the controls and making sure

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.170
they improve. However, I think we have come
to the conclusion that we need a pretty fundamental

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:09.010
look at the controls and the processes we
have around procurement.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:14.459
That is really for two reasons. One is that
I think it has been clear to us throughout

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:21.459
the year that we have not a set of controls
that is suitable for a complex system that

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.290
is managing budget of £96 million, etc. That
has certainly been clear to those of us who

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:28.620
have the privilege of sitting every Thursday
afternoon for an hour and a half looking at

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.350
stuff. It is just far too cumbersome the way
we have it set up.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:36.080
In part, that is due to external requirements
placed upon us under the efficiency controls.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.660
This committee is responsible, in part, for
those efficiency controls. However, it is

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:43.840
also the way we have set up our own internal
processes and financial arrangements. Under

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.199
the aegis of the organisational alignment
and capability programme, which you have talked

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:54.060
about already, we will therefore be doing
quite a major project to revisit all of that

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.169
and make sure we have something which has
the right balance.

00:04:56.169 --> 00:05:00.160
On the one hand, we want to be an excellent
organisation that controls its procurement

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.280
and makes sure the money is spent wisely.
On the other, we need to make sure that does

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.910
not hamper the organisation from doing the
things it needs to do. In so doing, we need

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:11.460
it to also address some of the things which
the audit committee has been looking at and

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.380
the internal auditors have looked at. Those
things are suggesting, similarly, that it

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:20.930
is not a fit for purpose regime. Secondly,
as a consequence of that in part, there has

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:25.400
been less than 100% compliance with that regime.
Clearly we need a regime which is fit for

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.979
purpose and is then complied with.

00:05:26.979 --> 00:05:31.680
So that is what the project is. It is going
to be a quick project. Therefore there will

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.400
be action over the next few months to put
all of that right.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:34.990
Malcolm Grant: Simon?

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:40.220
Simon Stevens: I would like to say another
way what Paul has just said. This kind of

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:45.590
exemplifies the issues we have about the scope
of authority that our area directors have.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:50.750
What is the relationship between what area
teams, regions, the national office and so

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.289
on are doing? Frankly, therefore, we have
to get more delegation of authority for a

00:05:54.289 --> 00:05:58.820
number of decisions that should be made by
senior frontline leaders. However, the quid

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:03.990
pro quo is, for those things where the delegation
of authority specifies national controls,

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:08.259
they have to be adhered to 100% without exception.

00:06:08.259 --> 00:06:10.650
Malcolm Grant: That is reasonably clear.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.930
Malcolm Grant: Good. Okay. Let us move on
to finance and investment. Moira?

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:19.930
Moira Gibb: I do not think there is anything
I want to tell the Board, Malcolm.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:26.930
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. Let us
move on to directly commissioned services.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.039
Victor?

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:32.470
Victor Adebowale: [Inaudible], Malcolm. [Inaudible]
fascinating.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:36.270
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Are there any other
comments.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:40.770
Victor Adebowale: I have nothing much to report
other than the comments that are in the minutes.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:46.199
That is other than to say I was really taken
aback by the sheer scale of the challenge,

00:06:46.199 --> 00:06:53.160
the complexity of it. I was very taken aback
by the fact we need to really think about

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.569
the effectiveness of the governance and the
efficiencies thereof.

00:06:56.569 --> 00:07:00.460
Simon Stevens: I do not want to be a broken
record. However, this is another example of

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.729
why, when it comes to specialised commissioning,
again we have the opportunity to think about

00:07:03.729 --> 00:07:08.310
what it is CCGs individually are collectively
are doing. All of this stuff should not end

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:13.389
up in a Board committee nationally. It is,
therefore, the corollary of the conversation

00:07:13.389 --> 00:07:15.180
we were having on efficiency controls.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:21.590
Malcolm Grant: To take Simon's point further,
he and I are quite committed over the summer

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:24.470
to reviewing the entire committee structure
for the Board.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:24.550
Victor Adebowale: Yes.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:28.870
Malcolm Grant: We wish to make sure the allocations
to frontline leaders are appropriate. We also

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:34.660
wish to ensure the decisions taken by the
executive team have appropriate lines of sight

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:39.340
to the Board. Finally, we wish to ensure the
Board committees are charged with responsibilities

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:43.300
that oversee, assure and relate to strategy.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:48.099
Victor Adebowale: Malcolm, again it is the
same question. The outcomes for this are a

00:07:48.099 --> 00:07:51.680
relevant feature for our governance.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:58.580
Malcolm Grant: Yes. Okay. On that note, perhaps
we can move on to Cyril on quality and clinical

00:07:58.580 --> 00:07:58.849
risk?

00:07:58.849 --> 00:08:05.169
Cyril Chantler: There is just one thing. We
reviewed, among other things, the work on

00:08:05.169 --> 00:08:10.389
incentives and patient safety. I would just
like to link those two together. It is a complex

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:17.389
area. However, our hope is that as incentives
are redesigned they will link into the NHS

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:25.470
outcomes framework. There they may be seen
to impact on improving the patient experience

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:32.380
and the patient safety. They may also reduce,
where possible, the [inaudible] mortality.

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:35.910
We will be discussing that more in September.
Thank you.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:39.769
Malcolm Grant: Comments? No? Thank you very
much.

00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:45.209
May I then move to congenital heart disease?
By the way, this is not a committee of the

00:08:45.209 --> 00:08:49.490
Board. It is a task and finish group. The
finishing line has moved a little further

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:51.860
away from where we had hoped it would be.

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:56.959
However, let me just very quickly set the
context for this. This is a review that follows

00:08:56.959 --> 00:09:03.959
some 15 years after Sir Ian Kennedy's review
of Bristol. I think we are still searching

00:09:04.709 --> 00:09:11.709
for a solution that will give us an acceptable
service with nationally and internationally

00:09:11.779 --> 00:09:17.959
acceptable specifications for children's heart
surgery and for congenital heart disease wrapped

00:09:17.959 --> 00:09:19.010
around that.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:25.640
It went through a quite complex and controversial
process called the Safe and Sustainable review.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:30.630
That subsequently came up with a set of recommendations.
They then went to the independent reconfiguration

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:37.450
panel. They raised objections which I think
were valid. They were sufficient to cause

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:43.180
us to decide not to proceed with Safe and
Sustainable but to start over again.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:47.740
That process kicked off a year ago. We had
hoped to be able, by the end of the year,

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:54.010
to bring a new set of proposals to the Board.
However, I think what has been commendable

00:09:54.010 --> 00:10:01.010
about the process is that it has been broadened
in its scope. It now extends to adult congenital

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.120
heart disease as well as children's congenital
heart disease. That was an issue raised by

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.209
the independent reconfiguration panel.

00:10:08.209 --> 00:10:15.209
It has also been extended in its scope to
look at the related services that a provider

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:21.370
must provide in order, competently, to handle
congenital heart disease. I think it has also

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:34.330
been a painstaking process to ensure all stakeholders
have been at the table and have been completed

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.070
informed as the discussions have gone on.
Those stakeholders include providers, families

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:43.100
and others. Therefore, I make no apology,
on behalf of the task and finish group, that

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:48.620
this is taking longer than we had originally
aspired to. I still have some confidence that

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:54.899
at the end of the process we will come up
with a set of national specifications that

00:10:54.899 --> 00:11:00.459
I think will be highly regarded and acceptable
across the piece.

00:11:00.459 --> 00:11:05.470
There is a full report of year one amongst
the papers. I know colleagues will have read

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:11.930
that. You will know from that we anticipate
going out to consultation in September with

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:17.269
a proposal that will help us to join up a
national view. This is not a matter of picking

00:11:17.269 --> 00:11:22.190
and choosing providers. This is a national
specification for what we want around congenital

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:25.930
heart disease. Furthermore, this comes at
a time when the incidence of congenital heart

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:31.029
disease is rising. The number of adults, or
children rising to adulthood, is of course

00:11:31.029 --> 00:11:37.029
also rising. Therefore, the context is already
different from that where the Safe and Sustainable

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:38.970
review set out originally.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:45.970
There is a full report in the papers and I
am happy to take any comments or questions

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:54.300
on it. No? In which case the only remaining
item under this heading, Jon Schick, is Board

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.029
committee annual reports.

00:11:55.029 --> 00:11:59.480
Jon Schick: Thank you. I would just like the
Board to agree the recommendations here. In

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:04.180
particular, there are some minor changes to
terms of reference of some of the committees.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:07.720
This will just see them through while we are
doing the review work that you mentioned earlier.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.839
They can carry on until we come up with a
new structure.

00:12:09.839 --> 00:12:13.250
Malcolm Grant: Right. Thank you all very much.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:18.519
Any other business: I have none except to
let you know of our next meeting. It is to

00:12:18.519 --> 00:12:25.519
be on 19th September. It will be also relating
to our annual general meeting. Last year we

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:32.959
held our first AGM of the NHS. This year I
know that there are some really interesting

00:12:32.959 --> 00:12:38.880
proposals being developed by Tim and his team
for engagement with patients and the full

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:45.399
engagement of all of the Board in a day of
work and learning.

00:12:45.399 --> 00:12:50.279
Unless anybody else has any other business,
I will close this part of the meeting. The

00:12:50.279 --> 00:12:55.610
Board will then move into private session
subsequently today to deal with matters on

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:58.970
that agenda. Thank you everybody for attending.
The meeting is closed.

